// ROME — CITIES, MONUMENTS AND ENGINEERING — full English content. Same structure and same image slots as the Portuguese version (dados.js).
// Conventional dates; legendary tradition is flagged as such. Figures (population, capacities, lengths) are estimates.

const visao = [
  { caixa: 'In brief', texto: [
    'The Romans were, above all, **builders**. In a thousand years they turned a village of hills beside the Tiber into a city of about a million people, and scattered from the Atlantic to the Euphrates hundreds of cities built on the same logic: a **forum** at the centre, **temples** and **basilicas**, **baths**, **theatres** and **amphitheatres**, a grid of streets, **aqueducts** bringing water in and **sewers** taking it away.',
    'This page is about those cities and about the **engineering** that made them possible: concrete, the arch and the dome, aqueducts, roads and bridges. It walks through Rome (Forum, Colosseum, Pantheon), the cities buried by Vesuvius (Pompeii and Herculaneum), the port of Ostia, the cities of the provinces and, with special attention, **Hispania and Lusitania**: Conímbriga, Mérida, Évora, Tróia, Lisbon. For political history see the chronological pages; for the army that also built, the Army page; for gods, law and everyday life, the page “Religion, law and daily life”.'
  ] },
  { img: 'rce-coliseu-exterior', leg: 'Colosseum (Flavian Amphitheatre), Rome: exterior view.' },
  { h: 'Where and when' },
  'The city of Rome was born, according to tradition, in 753 BC; archaeology shows villages on the hills from the 9th century BC, and a drained and paved Forum around 600 BC. The great building period runs from the **middle Republic** (4th–2nd centuries BC) to the **end of the Empire** (4th century AD), with its peak between **Augustus and the Antonines** (27 BC–AD 192). The table gives the essentials.',
  { tabela: { cab: ['Phase', 'Dates', 'What is built'], linhas: [
    ['Archaic Rome', 'c. 800 – 509 BC', 'Drainage of the Forum, Cloaca Maxima (according to tradition), first temples, tufa walls'],
    ['Republic', '509 – 27 BC', 'Via Appia and Aqua Appia (312), arched aqueducts, roads across Italy, spread of concrete, first stone theatre in Rome (55)'],
    ['High Empire', '27 BC – AD 192', 'Augustus’ “marble” Rome, Colosseum, imperial fora, Pantheon, great aqueducts, Romanised cities in the provinces'],
    ['Late Empire', 'AD 193 – 476', 'Baths of Caracalla and Diocletian, Aurelian Wall, Basilica of Maxentius, imperial palaces, city walls in the provinces'],
    ['After 476', '5th–6th centuries onwards', 'Aqueducts cut (537), shrunken cities, ruins reused as quarries, churches or fortresses']
  ] } },
  { h: 'A civilisation of cities' },
  'The Roman Empire was a federation of **cities** that governed the territory around them. Rome founded **colonies** (cities of veterans and settlers) and raised local communities to **municipia**, each with its own magistrates, council and forum. This model, copied from Rome, explains why so many European cities, from London to Cologne, from Lisbon to Barcelona, have Roman origins, and why the plans of Conímbriga, Timgad or Pompeii look so alike.',
  { img: 'rce-forum-romano', leg: 'Roman Forum viewed from the Capitoline Hill, with the Colosseum in the background.' },
  { h: 'Why this page matters' },
  { lista: [
    '**Almost everything still standing** from the Roman world is public works: roads, bridges, aqueducts, theatres, temples.',
    '**Roman engineering** solved problems (supplying a city of a million with water, crossing rivers, roofing huge spaces) with solutions that were not surpassed for many centuries.',
    '**Pompeii and Herculaneum** are the most direct window we have onto daily life in a Roman city, frozen in AD 79.',
    '**In Portugal,** the Roman past is in plain sight: from Conímbriga to Tróia, from the bridge at Chaves to the Roman theatre in Lisbon.',
    '**Western architecture** (arches, vaults, domes, squares, baths, law courts) owes its vocabulary to Rome.'
  ] },
  { h: 'Engineering in the service of the state' },
  'The Romans seldom invented techniques from scratch (the arch, the vault and piped water already existed in Etruria, Greece and the Near East), but they were masters at **combining, standardising and carrying them out on a huge scale**. They had a state able to mobilise soldiers, slaves, free workers and contractors (the *publicani*), a practical idea of “public works” as both service and propaganda, and an almost magical material: **concrete**.',
  { img: 'rce-pont-du-gard', leg: 'Pont du Gard, aqueduct bridge near Nîmes.' },
  { h: 'How we know' },
  'We know Rome’s built world through three channels. First, **texts**: the treatise *On Architecture* by **Vitruvius** (1st century BC), Frontinus’ book on the aqueducts of Rome (c. AD 97), the letters of **Pliny the Younger**, the descriptions of Strabo and Cassius Dio. Second, **archaeology**: from ruins to the excavations at Pompeii, Ostia or Conímbriga. Third, **epigraphy**, inscriptions in stone and bronze that say who paid for a bridge, who rebuilt a theatre, how many miles separate two cities. Each source has its limits: Vitruvius is a theorist, Frontinus writes about Rome and not the provinces, and many buildings were altered over the centuries.',
  { h: 'Legend and fact' },
  { lista: [
    '**Nero and the lyre.** There is no proof that Nero played the lyre while watching Rome burn in AD 64 (Tacitus says it was a rumour); he was in fact away from the city when the fire began.',
    '**“Vomitoria”.** The *vomitoria* of the Colosseum and the theatres were the passages through which the crowd “spilled out” onto the stairs; they were not rooms where Romans vomited in order to keep eating.',
    '**Martyrs in the Colosseum.** Christians were executed in Rome, but there is no firm evidence of mass martyrdoms in the Colosseum; the tradition is largely later.',
    '**753 BC and Romulus.** The date and the founders are tradition; archaeology confirms villages on the hills and a civic centre taking shape in the 8th–6th centuries BC.',
    '**The “lost” concrete.** The technique fell out of use for centuries, but it is not a lost secret: the basic recipe (lime, pozzolana and stone) is well understood today, and what is debated is detail.'
  ] },
  { img: 'rce-via-appia', leg: 'Via Appia Antica with basalt paving near Rome.' },
  { h: 'Rome at its height' },
  'Around AD 120 the city had about **a million inhabitants** (estimates range from 450,000 to 1.2 million), ten aqueducts, hundreds of baths and fountains, a Colosseum, a Pantheon, marble fora and districts of multi-storey apartment blocks. No other city in Europe again had this population until the early 19th century.',
  { img: 'rce-roma-panorama-ia', leg: 'AI-generated illustration. Rome viewed from the Palatine, c. AD 120: artistic interpretation.' },
  { h: 'Today' },
  'The Historic Centre of Rome, the Pont du Gard, Pompeii and Herculaneum, Mérida, Tarragona, Évora and many other sites are UNESCO **World Heritage**. The Appian Way was inscribed in 2024. In Portugal, Conímbriga is the largest excavated Roman site, and the bridge at Alcántara, the bridge at Chaves and the Roman theatre in Lisbon remain a living part of the landscape. This page walks through them with cautious dates, marking what is certain, what is probable and what remains debated.'
];

const linha = [
  'Conventional dates, in the usual chronology of Rome. The first part (to the 6th century BC) mixes tradition and archaeology; traditional dates are flagged as such. For the political history of each period, see the other Rome pages.',
  { linha: [
    { d: 'c. 800 – 600 BC', t: 'Hills, marshes and a Forum', x: 'Hut villages on the Palatine, Capitoline and Quirinal; the marshy valley of the future Forum is used as a cemetery and then drained and paved (c. 600 BC, according to archaeology). Tradition ties the work to the Etruscan kings, **Tarquinius Priscus** in particular.' },
    { d: '6th c. BC (tradition)', t: 'Cloaca Maxima', x: 'The great collector that drains the Forum into the Tiber is attributed, according to Livy, to **Tarquinius Priscus** or to Tarquin the Proud. The original drain was probably an open channel; the stone vault visible today is partly much later (2nd–1st centuries BC). It is still partly in use.' },
  ] },
  { img: 'rce-cloaca-maxima', leg: 'Cloaca Maxima outlet into the Tiber, Rome.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: '509 BC (tradition)', t: 'Temple of Jupiter Capitolinus', x: 'According to tradition, the great Etruscan temple on the Capitoline was dedicated in the first year of the Republic. Archaeology confirms a huge temple in the late 6th century BC, the largest in Italy of its time.' },
    { d: 'c. 378 BC', t: 'The “Servian” wall', x: 'After the sack of Rome by the Gauls (c. 390 BC), the city builds a wall of tufa blocks about 11 km long. Tradition attributed it to King Servius Tullius, but the wall that survives dates from the 4th century BC.' },
    { d: '312 BC', t: 'Via Appia and Aqua Appia', x: 'The censor **Appius Claudius Caecus** begins the first great Roman road (Rome–Capua, later extended to Brundisium) and the city’s first aqueduct, mostly underground, about 16 km long.' },
    { d: '272 – 269 BC', t: 'Anio Vetus', x: 'The second aqueduct, about 64 km long, brings water from the river Anio. The money comes from the spoils of the war against Pyrrhus.' },
    { d: 'c. 220 BC', t: 'Via Flaminia', x: 'The censor Gaius Flaminius has the road opened that links Rome to the Adriatic (Ariminum, today Rimini). The Via Aemilia (187 BC) follows and, in the provinces, the Via Domitia (118 BC, Gaul) and the Via Egnatia (2nd century BC, Balkans).' },
    { d: '144 – 140 BC', t: 'Aqua Marcia', x: 'The longest aqueduct of the Republic (about 91 km) has a stretch of arches several kilometres long at the approach to the city, and raises the water high enough to supply the hills, including the Capitoline.' },
    { d: '2nd – 1st c. BC', t: 'Roman concrete becomes general', x: '*Opus caementicium* (a mortar of lime and pozzolana with stones) goes from an occasional technique to the basis of construction, first in Rome and Campania. It allows vaults and structures without precedent. Architects continue to face it with stone, brick or marble.' },
    { d: '55 BC', t: 'Theatre of Pompey', x: 'The first permanent stone theatre in Rome. To get round the tradition that forbade lasting theatres, Pompey placed a small temple of Venus Victrix at the top of the seating, so that the steps looked like the temple’s stairway.' },
    { d: 'c. 80 BC', t: 'Pompeii becomes a Roman colony', x: 'After the Social War and Sulla’s siege, the Oscan-speaking town receives Roman settlers. The amphitheatre (c. 70 BC) is the oldest surviving stone amphitheatre.' },
    { d: '46 – 44 BC', t: 'Caesar and the Forum of Caesar', x: 'Julius Caesar begins a new forum next to the old one, with the temple of Venus Genetrix, and plans a building programme that Augustus will complete: theatre, library, new roads.' },
    { d: '25 BC', t: 'Foundation of Augusta Emerita', x: 'Augustus founds, for veterans of the Cantabrian wars, the future capital of **Lusitania**: Mérida. The theatre (16–15 BC) was raised on Agrippa’s initiative. In Rome, Agrippa builds the first great baths (25 BC).' },
    { d: '27 – 19 BC', t: 'Agrippa: Pantheon and Aqua Virgo', x: 'Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa, Augustus’ son-in-law, builds the first Pantheon (27–25 BC, earlier than the present one), the Aqua Virgo (19 BC) and oversees the maintenance of the water network. According to Suetonius, Augustus claimed to have found Rome a city of brick and to leave it a city of marble.' },
    { d: '7 BC – AD 6', t: 'Rome divided into 14 regions; the vigiles', x: 'Augustus divides the city into 14 regions and in AD 6 creates the *vigiles*, firefighters and night police, after several serious fires.' },
    { d: 'AD 64 – 68', t: 'The fire and the Domus Aurea', x: 'In July AD 64 a fire destroys much of Rome over six to nine days. Nero rebuilds with new rules (wider streets, porticoes, height limits, more stone and less timber) and raises the **Domus Aurea**, his vast urban villa. The rumours about Nero’s responsibility have no proof.' },
  ] },
  { img: 'rce-incendio-roma-ia', leg: 'AI-generated illustration. Great Fire of Rome, AD 64: artistic interpretation.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: 'c. AD 70 – 80', t: 'The Colosseum', x: 'Vespasian begins the **Flavian Amphitheatre** on the site of the artificial lake of the Domus Aurea; Titus inaugurates it in 80, and Domitian adds the hypogeum and the top storey.' },
    { d: 'AD 79', t: 'Vesuvius buries Pompeii and Herculaneum', x: 'The eruption of AD 79, described by **Pliny the Younger**, buries the cities of the Bay of Naples. The traditional date of 24 August is now questioned by a charcoal inscription found in 2018 and by studies pointing to October.' },
    { d: 'c. AD 97 – 113', t: 'Frontinus and Trajan', x: 'Frontinus writes on the aqueducts of Rome (c. 97). Under Trajan the architect **Apollodorus of Damascus** designs the Forum, the Markets and the Column (dedicated in 113); the Danube bridge (105), the Aqua Traiana (109) and the Alcántara bridge (104–106) are built.' },
  ] },
  { img: 'rce-coluna-trajano', leg: 'Trajan’s Column, Rome (AD 113).' },
  { linha: [
    { d: 'c. AD 114 – c. 128', t: 'Hadrian’s Pantheon', x: 'The present temple of the Pantheon dates from Hadrian’s time, with its concrete dome 43.3 m in diameter. Brick stamps point to construction under Hadrian, perhaps begun under Trajan.' },
    { d: 'AD 212 – 216', t: 'Baths of Caracalla', x: 'A bath complex for hundreds of people, supplied by the new branch Aqua Antoniniana. It is inaugurated in 216.' },
    { d: 'AD 271 – 275', t: 'The Aurelian Wall', x: 'Facing the invasions of the 3rd century, Rome, which had had no walls since the Republic, is surrounded by a new wall about 19 km long, incorporating existing buildings such as the Amphitheatrum Castrense and the pyramid of Cestius.' },
    { d: 'c. AD 295 – 306', t: 'Diocletian: baths and palace', x: 'The Baths of Diocletian (inaugurated c. 306) are the largest in Rome. At Split (Dalmatia), Diocletian builds the palace where he will live after abdicating in 305.' },
    { d: 'c. AD 308 – 315', t: 'Basilica of Maxentius and Arch of Constantine', x: 'The great basilica, begun by Maxentius and finished by Constantine, and the triumphal arch dedicated to Constantine in 315, next to the Colosseum, are among the last great monuments of pagan Rome.' },
  ] },
  { img: 'rce-arco-constantino', leg: 'Arch of Constantine beside the Colosseum, Rome.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: 'AD 410 – 537', t: 'Sack, fall and siege', x: 'Rome is sacked by Alaric’s Goths in 410 and by the Vandals in 455; the Western Empire ends in 476 (conventional date). In 537, during the Gothic War, Vitiges’ Goths cut the aqueducts, and the baths stop working.' },
    { d: '1349 – 1863', t: 'Ruin and rediscovery', x: 'The earthquake of 1349 brings down part of the Colosseum; the ruins serve as quarry, church or fortress. Herculaneum has been excavated since 1738 and Pompeii since 1748; **Giuseppe Fiorelli** introduces in 1863 the plaster-cast method and systematic excavation.' }
  ] }
];

